cup (-2,0),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/fcc9f998111493167284cf5844645ac2201a230b073a9336604fb790ba16bad4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0a521b996073340092129ba3d68a5908b5afbd5caf9ed3992ee58c7815067fcb"} {"question" "q-98040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/03d9436c922b602b3e3388dfc21b6e8c20aae96f70b378de706b3c96b7ded253.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/86bcece5228ba30310e4b4ed663c5efcb551609474fa924034efac08aa055a23.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/d46036d0f5c0fc689d1123e3512a446b94688f6fa94df017ee26682860ed6b64.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f22dc056accfeae38eea6fb0207adfe4676bd8bd875cbeb96c078f4eb6457a6e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f95aa207248151fc7035fcd6dcdea8de58ebff368aa886b18413249bf30225ba.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f22dc056accfeae38eea6fb0207adfe4676bd8bd875cbeb96c078f4eb6457a6e.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0a521b996073340092129ba3d68a5908b5afbd5caf9ed3992ee58c7815067fcb"} {"question" "q-98632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"} {"question" "q-98632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1c0ecf9d7f8f6de9b586399f6fc5624c670d72abd803bb02ee87e86350cb413"}] "usage" {"q-96513" [] "q-96516" []} "basis" 1961 "retractions" [[17592186148163 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96513.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n1. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2} - 1$\n2. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y - 1)^{2}(y + 1)$\n3. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y - 1)^{2}$"] [17592186148166 :q